#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "b293a58f-0903-5ee7-ab4b-3cbc2e5e4b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-560" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/760/Source/Images/d295aff6e58a4512c5e355673a1589a0.png)" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^{3/2}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1 + [\\frac{3}{2}x^{1/2}]^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4 + 9x}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4 + 9x)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sqrt {1 + [f'(x)]^2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}\n$$\n\nto the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu= 4+9x\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{9}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}∫_{4}^{16}u^{\\frac{1}{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}[\\frac{2}{3}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}]_{4}^{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}] - [(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{27}([64] - [8]) \\\\\n&= \\frac{56}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "cf9adf09-b14f-5b5b-a110-60354b93c241" :question/math-academy-id "e-561" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle \\int_{5}^{6}} \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}} \\,\\textrm{d}x$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?\n\n1. $y= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n2. $y= \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n3. $y= \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6} \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= ± ∫\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 16}}dx \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 16} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is\n\n$$\ny= \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/id #uuid "80794b09-e0f4-5bff-8c0c-8261638f9613" :question/math-academy-id "e-7268" :question/problem "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]$?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\arccos{x}\n$$\n\n$a=0$, and\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nf'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\ninto the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1 + [f^{′}(x)]^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{1 - x^{2}}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1 - x^{2} + 1}{1 - x^{2}}}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x^{2}}{1 - x^{2}}}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d75b42bd-402a-532a-9832-53137a5a026a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}\\cot xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080055} {:answer/id #uuid "0e856201-f798-58ed-a6c0-79560574c31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}\\sin xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080056} {:answer/id #uuid "23412a2f-eeef-5dce-bc21-01cfc2cb7afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}\\csc xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080057} {:answer/id #uuid "24577e37-27ed-5446-b93e-ef5dedf062eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}\\cos xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080058} {:answer/id #uuid "5da0ec32-4a8e-53f5-bca1-b69fce0525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}\\tan xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23412a2f-eeef-5dce-bc21-01cfc2cb7afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}\\csc xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080057} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8202ab-3d9a-5650-90cc-45887953205a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080054}] :question/id #uuid "8bbaf62f-484c-5d9e-8854-24692016788e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15397" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y = \\ln (\\csc x)$ on $[1, 3]$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88a1b1e6-87cf-5f0e-89e3-cea5b1fadb89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{4x^{3}}{3} - 2x^{2} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082473} {:answer/id #uuid "ba201ae1-3784-5f25-b538-a3db1c7fd099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082474} {:answer/id #uuid "f883ee93-d216-5bdf-9ce7-a5943782e6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082475} {:answer/id #uuid "f9187eac-d0d4-5321-8870-9cbe1376088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - x + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082476} {:answer/id #uuid "74f5f5fe-3fd4-5fcb-81a6-e26c31bad4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9187eac-d0d4-5321-8870-9cbe1376088d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{2} - x + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082476} :answer-field/id #uuid "80edbeb1-18ba-5dd9-9b3f-f1d655ee4147" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082472}] :question/id #uuid "ab3de3f1-6311-5aac-86d4-b283aa079450" :question/math-academy-id "q-82624" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y = f(x)$ is given by $∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2} - 4x}dx$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?"}]}
